The $$H-X$$ bond strength decreases from $$HF$$ to $$HI.$$ i.e. $$HF > HCl > HBr > HI.$$ Thus $$HF$$ is most stable while $$HI$$ is least stable. This is evident from their decompositions reaction: $$HF$$ and $$HCl$$ are stable upto $$1473K,HBr$$ decreases slightly and $$HI$$ dissociates considerably $$713K.$$ The decreasing stability of the hydrogen halide is also reflected in the values of dissociation energy of the $$H-X$$ bond

$$\matrix{
{H - F} & {H - Cl} & {H - Br} & {H - I} \cr
{135kcal\,mo{l^{ - 1}}} & {103kcal\,mo{l^{ - 1}}} & {87kcal\,mo{l^{ - 1}}} & {71kcal\,mo{l^{ - 1}}} \cr

} $$

